course: dictionary
lesson: 2115
title: Convenient and its many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. Convenient can mean easy to use or reach, suitable for someone's plans, or near and easy to get to. Read the sentence around it and choose the meaning that fits best.
explain: «The myth was convenient, and convenient myths can hide unkindness.» Here convenient means suitable for someone's plans. A convenient myth suits what somebody wants, even when it is not true. The clue is that the myth is useful to someone, so it has to suit their plans.
explain: For everyday things, convenient usually means easy to get to or use. A convenient time for a meeting is one that suits everyone, and a convenient shop is one near your home. Ask yourself: is this thing easy to reach or use, or does it suit someone's plans? The answer shows you the meaning.
fact: Convenient means easy to use, reach or do, or suitable for someone's plans.
fact: «Convenient» can also mean near and easy to get to, as in a convenience store.
fact: «Conveniently» is the adverb, as in «He conveniently forgot his homework», which means he forgot in a way that suited him.
fact: Convenience is the noun, as in «convenience food», which is food that saves time and trouble.
fact: A good check is to swap in your meaning, such as "suited his plans", and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: In «The myth was convenient, and convenient myths can hide unkindness», what does convenient mean? | Very old and well known | Loud and noisy | Full of bright colours | *Suitable for someone's plans
exercise: Mum says the shop near our house is very convenient. What does convenient mean here? | Closed on Sundays | Full of old books | *Near and easy to get to | Very expensive
exercise: Find the adverb in «He conveniently forgot his homework». | forgot | *conveniently | He | homework
test: «We chose a convenient time for the meeting, when everyone was free.» What does convenient mean here? | Wet and grey | Very old | Painful and slow | *Suitable for everyone's plans
test: Convenience food is food that is | Only eaten in winter | Sour and bitter | Very old and rare | *Quick and easy to prepare
exam: Why should a reader be careful when a convenient myth appears in a story? | *Because it may suit someone's plans and hide the truth | Because it means the story is about food | Because it is written in a different language | Because it is always a true fact
